On Dec 22, 12:01 am, Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com>
wrote:

> I think you cannot prevent leaking [some] information.

In principle, I am not convinced that it would even be
necessary to prevent the leaking of any information at all
ever.  I think it would be quite adequate to just reduce the
impact so that it is of largely minor passing interest and
not any kind of major consequential factor.

I mean, NetHack leaks information to the player in all kinds
of different ways.  Pet testing, watching monster behavior
in general, price checking, dip testing, frequency analysis,
repeated dropping and picking up (to rule out scare
monster), etc.  Yes, I realize these are all in-game events,
and another game's dump logs are out-of-game information,
but it remains that there are a LOT of different sources of
information in NetHack, as well there should be.  If dump
logs from another game often provide a disproportionally
large *amount* of information (which may be the case
currently), then I agree that is a problem.  If, however,
they provided a much smaller amount of information, making
them just one among many small sources of little bits
information here and there, then I believe that would be a
satisfactory outcome, overall.

I think it is enough to substantially lessen the information
that they provide (about the items in your shiny new pile).

So, for example, the patch that re-orders the items in the
bones pile so they don't necessarily match the order in the
dump log is a good step, IMO.  Perhaps there are other steps
that could be taken as well, along similar lines.

> And we don't want restrictions WRT accessibility of
> dumplogs; they are used as reference in postings, they are
> used to do statistical evaluations, and they can be (at
> least partly) even educational in conjunction with the
> ttyrecs.

I absolutely agree with this.


